            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Illness and Death</strong><br>\nAnne's health began to decline in 1848, and she died of tuberculosis on May 28, 1849. Her death came after witnessing the deaths of her siblings, Emily and Branwell, to the same disease.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nAlthough Anne's work was initially overshadowed by that of her sisters, her novels have gained recognition for their boldness and the bravery of her opinions in the face of restrictive social norms.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nAnne Brontë remains an important figure in literature, her novels lauded for their feminist stance and contribution to discussions about the role of women in society and the right to an independent life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
